    <statement><p>Calculate the integral:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle \int \frac{1}{(x+2)(x+6)}\,dx =</m>
<fillin characters="55" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0.25\ln\mathopen{}\left(\left|x+2\right|\right)-0.25\ln\mathopen{}\left(\left|x+6\right|\right)+C</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><me>\frac{1}{(x+2)(x+6)}=\frac{A}{x+2}+\frac{B}{x+6},</me>
giving
<me>1 =  A(x+6)+B(x+2),</me>
or
<me>1 = (A+B)x+ (6 A+2 B).</me>
Solving for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, we find <m>A = 0.25</m> and <m>B = -0.25</m>,
so
<me>\int \frac{1}{(x+2)(x+6)}\,dx= \int\frac{0.25}{x+2}\,dx +
  \int \frac{-0.25}{x+6}\,dx = 0.25\ln\mathopen{}\left(\left|x+2\right|\right)-0.25\ln\mathopen{}\left(\left|x+6\right|\right)+C.</me>



</solution>
